HOW TO COMPLETE (for the reader)
On any machine that can reach maayanlab.cloud: (1) copy the data/ and analysis/ directories,
(2) `pip install gseapy`, (3) `python3 /workspace/analysis/run_enrichment.py`. The script loads
analysis/de_gene_symbols.txt, calls gseapy.enrich(gene_list=..., gene_sets="Reactome_2022",
organism="human") with Enrichr's default background (falling back to the same Enrichr REST endpoints
/addList + /enrich if the package is missing), writes analysis/enrichr_reactome2022_full_results.csv,
prints every Term matching p53 / tp53 / cell cycle / G1/S / DNA damage with its Odds Ratio, P-value,
adjusted P-value, overlap and gene count, names the primary row by the agreed tie-break (prefer the term
that most directly reads as "p53-mediated regulation of cell cycle genes", e.g. a "G1/S Specific
Transcriptional Regulation by P53" / "Regulation of G1/S Transition" style term over generic "Cell Cycle"
parents; remaining ties by smallest P-value) and reports that row's Enrichr `Odds Ratio` as the answer.
Then repeat with `--gene-list analysis/de_symbols_t_df4.txt` (1116 symbols) to see how the DE core's
liberalness moves the OR; the headline remains the 1680-symbol raw-p list by protocol. A third option,
`--local-gmt <Reactome_2022.gmt>`, uses gseapy's offline mode but is only valid if gseapy imports, and its
output is deliberately named *_APPROXIMATION.csv because a local gmt is not the Enrichr service.

INTERPRETATION NOTE ON THE QUERY/BACKGROUND FRACTION
The query is 1680 symbols = 5.7% of the 29540 testable genes in this contrast (2.6% of the 63,677
matrix rows). Enrichr's default background (background=None) is the whole gene-set library universe rather
than our expressed subset, so a query covering ~6% of the transcriptome sits inside a background that
already contains a large share of every pathway's members; Fisher odds ratios from a list this size are
expected to be modest (roughly ~1.1-2 for the leading terms of a real but broad transcriptional response)
rather than the 5-20 typical of small, tightly targeted queries. Judge the OR together with the P-value,
adjusted P-value and Overlap columns of the result table, not in isolation.
